## Summary

Before: the PDF client tool stored only a first-page thumbnail, and no page images were stored as child documents.
Now: the tool asynchronously renders every page using client-side Poppler, uploads each PNG as a child DOCUMENT backed by deduplicated CONTENT, and uploads a separately scaled first-page thumbnail. The completed TODO item for #179 is removed.

Ian explicitly corrected the original pure-Go renderer requirement: rendering belongs on a client machine, with no renderer or native dependency in the server. An initial server renderer approach was fully removed. The server upload endpoint validates source identity and consecutive page numbering, supports idempotent retries, and records completion only after the final page. Source replacement clears stale page children and rendering metadata. Originals and transcription-range PDFs each retain their own page children.

Production census found 1,199 PDF document appearances, yielding 13,369 page children and at most 22,650 new CONTENT rows before deduplication. Rendering 649 real pages across 33 PDFs took 226 seconds and produced 444 MB of PNGs; projected rendering is about 59 minutes, with 2.8–6.0 GB of stored PNGs depending on deduplication. Upload time is additional. Client/database tests and CGO-disabled vet passed, including the oversized-thumbnail regression. The requested merge from main was already up to date; checks passed again with no conflicts or edits.

The authorized production schema migration completed: createtabs added DOCUMENT.page_count and DOCUMENT.rendered. Deployment and the client backfill were deliberately left for after merge; neither has run.

